.TH STRALLOC_CATSET 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
stralloc_catset \- Append the contents of a set to a string allocation with a separator

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/str.h>
.IB #include <lib/stralloc.h>

.IB size_t
.IB stralloc_catset(stralloc* sa, const set_t* set, const char* separator);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBstralloc_catset\fR function appends the contents of a set to a string allocation with a separator. It takes three arguments: the string allocation to append to, the set to append, and the separator to use. The function returns the number of characters appended.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB sa
The string allocation to append to.
.TP
.IB set
The set to append.
.TP
.IB separator
The separator to use.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of characters appended.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBstralloc_catset\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/str.h>
#include <lib/stralloc.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    stralloc sa;
    set_init(&set);
    stralloc_init(&sa);

    set_inserts(&set, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set, "cherry");

    printf("Set: %u elements\n", set.len);

    stralloc_catset(&sa, &set, ", ");

    printf("String allocation: %s\n", sa.s);

    set_free(&set);
    stralloc_free(&sa);
    return 0;
}
.nef